00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:27.100
AMY BANZAERT:
There were a couple

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.730
of questions on estimations.

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:31.780
So one person asked for
answers to estimations.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:34.900
I try to give you orders of
magnitude and give you a sense,

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:40.000
but if I missed some, if you
want to email me and be like,

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.700
hey, I never really got
this one, let me know,

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.565
and I can review them.

00:00:44.565 --> 00:00:45.940
And then also,
the other thing is

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.870
I'm never going to give you a
6.7 answer because I'm really

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:52.870
asking you an estimation
that has that kind of answer.

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:56.620
Like, some of them
do, but usually it's

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.770
not even that important
that you get to 6.7.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:02.290
It's important that you get to
an order of magnitude of 10.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:03.970
And as long as you're
there, I'm happy,

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:05.170
and so that's why
I'm not getting

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:06.545
to the exact
answer, which I know

00:01:06.545 --> 00:01:10.930
is uncomfortable and not what
you're used to educationally,

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:13.600
but it's what the
estimation is there for.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.300
Another person mentioned
that some people

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.280
seem like they were
taking a really long time

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:20.935
and getting lost
in the estimations.

00:01:20.935 --> 00:01:22.810
So I try to give you
the right amount of time

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.740
for the estimation so
that it's reasonable,

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.840
but it is something to practice
getting more facile with,

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:30.340
I guess.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:33.680
All right, so wind energy.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.535
So does anyone know why
we have wind on the Earth?

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:40.710
Yeah.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:42.300
AUDIENCE: Because we have heat.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.910
AMY BANZAERT: Yes, from?

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:44.670
AUDIENCE: The Sun.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.380
AMY BANZAERT: Yes so
it's kind of funny

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.740
because wind energy is basically
a funky solar energy, which

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.780
I find pleasing.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.150
I don't know why, but I do.

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.880
There's also some Coriolis
effect stuff going around

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.620
on with the Earth rotating,
but the indirect solar energy

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:08.979
component is more
easy to think about

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:11.520
and I think is the bigger
component, though I'm not

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.150
sure of that.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:17.850
So basically, you have
uneven heating of the Earth,

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:21.960
hot air rises, causes a pressure
drop, cold air rushes in,

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.370
and that movement is what
we're getting in wind.

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:31.810
So that's what's going on there.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:34.110
And then a lot of
confusion sometimes

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:38.130
happens-- like, a lot of people
see these turbines, or not

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:40.230
turbines, but mills on farms.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:41.100
And it's confusing.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.645
Like, what's this versus this?

00:02:42.645 --> 00:02:44.680
Is this just a junky
version of this?

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.730
And so the difference
is, if it's a mill,

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:50.970
it's extracting mechanical
power, versus a turbine

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.870
is extracting electrical power.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:59.220
So these mills are very
high-torque devices

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:03.990
that move relatively slowly
in terms of their rotation

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:07.320
and are used to do things like
pump water or grind grain.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.000
They've been around forever.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.770
They're still really
common in the US

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.502
and in many places
for pumping water.

00:03:12.502 --> 00:03:13.960
So if you drive
across the country,

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.920
you'll see these everywhere.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.510
And they're still
in use, and they're

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:21.960
really nice and a whole
lot cheaper to build a mill

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.210
than to build a
turbine because you're

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.370
going from mechanical
work to mechanical work.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.040
So that's very direct
and much, much cheaper

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.510
than doing the extra steps
to get from mechanical work

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:36.010
to electric power generation.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:39.780
So a wind turbine, in
contrast, is electrical power.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:44.700
They have been around a long
time too, but not basically

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:48.630
for most of humanity long time.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:53.400
And again, they're going
to be moving very quickly

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.500
at much lower torque.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.780
The solidity thing
to think about

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.820
is that when you
want high torque,

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.980
you want to be getting
as much of the air

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:04.570
hitting your blades as possible.

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:07.620
So that's why there's often
many, many blades or really

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:11.280
thick blades on a windmill,
whereas a wind turbine,

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.440
you want low
solidity, so you want

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.560
a low proportion of your blade
to be in that swept area.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.682
So that's why a turbine is--
it looks very different.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:29.150
So wind turbines, I'm
going to focus on,

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:32.035
but I will go back to windmills
a little bit at the end.

00:04:36.285 --> 00:04:37.440
You have two types, right?

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.350
The horizontal and
the vertical axis.

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:41.790
The horizontal
axis is what you're

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:46.740
used to seeing around here
and what's in the news a lot

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:50.310
because they can go to
much higher heights,

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:55.390
and wind is much better the
higher you go, basically.

00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:59.010
And so the horizontal
wind axis turbines

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:04.350
are the more common thing,
at least in my knowledge

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:06.420
before I started doing
any research into them.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:11.050
So there's the rotor and
the motor are at the top.

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:14.790
It has to point into the wind.

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:17.190
The higher you go,
the better, but you

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:22.200
can imagine the higher you
go adds cost and difficulty.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:25.380
You often try to
change your blade pitch

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.900
to maximize the efficiency
because, depending

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:32.220
on how fast the wind is, you
want a different blade pitch.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:35.190
And they're now
really insanely huge.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:44.440
[LAUGHS] So this is an insanely
huge 7-megawatt turbine,

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:50.310
which I think you
can see-- like,

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:52.050
is there a truck down
there or anything?

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:54.180
It's hard to get scale
when it gets this big,

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:55.377
but it's ridiculous.

00:05:55.377 --> 00:05:57.210
And some of the challenges,
you can imagine,

00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:59.742
in building this thing is
just getting all the materials

00:05:59.742 --> 00:06:01.200
to the site because
you're probably

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:02.742
not going to build
your manufacturing

00:06:02.742 --> 00:06:04.310
plant of how you build your--

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:10.140
I'm still a little off--

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:12.300
that long blade, thank you.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.490
[LAUGHS]

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:15.990
You're not going to build your
blade right on the site where

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:18.060
you're going to want to
install your turbine, probably,

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:19.768
so then you have to
get your blade there.

00:06:19.768 --> 00:06:23.700
So then you're actually limited
by the size of a train railroad

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:27.310
car or a large
truck or whatever.

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:29.400
So there's all these
crazy limits when you're

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:33.330
getting up to be this enormous.

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:35.910
So it's kind of remarkable
and challenging,

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:38.700
and this is of a scale that
we're not going to be touching,

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:43.440
of course, but it's nice
to see the extreme of what

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.160
is out there.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:47.580
So this is a 7-megawatt
turbine that's actually

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:50.070
running now, I believe,
and they're currently

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:53.880
building a 10-megawatt that'll
be in production in a year

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:57.060
or running in a year
or two, so which

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:58.830
is a pretty big jump, 7 to 10.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:06.130
So, yeah, it's definitely
an area that's taking off.

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:09.480
So how?

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:13.050
This is just the classic
Bernoulli drawing

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:15.600
to remind you how they're
spinning in the wind

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:19.410
and that the motion is
really dominated by the lift,

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:22.080
not drag.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:29.760
One thing to think about is the
need to control the spin rate.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:35.280
So you can imagine on days
where there's not much wind,

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:37.620
you might want to change
the pitch of the blade

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:41.520
to make sure you're maximizing
the wind you're capturing.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.650
But on hurricane days,
you have to make sure

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:50.880
that your wind turbine doesn't
completely just spin out

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:51.850
of control.

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:55.330
And so this is a really nice,
relatively simple way to do it.

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:58.650
So you can do it
mechanically or electrically,

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.840
or you can do it manually.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:07.620
So with old windmills
from 100 or 200 years ago,

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.360
there was some
poor guy whose job

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:13.440
it was to turn the windmill
into the wind or not,

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:14.370
depending on how fast.

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:17.310
It was so thankfully,
there's been

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:23.110
ways to optimize that either
mechanically or electrically.

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:29.640
So this is a method where
basically the weight

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:34.320
of the tail and the
turbine allows the tail

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:39.960
to stay connected to the
turbine so that the tail--

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:43.080
I'm getting lots of confused
faces, so I'll go slowly.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:46.860
So the turbine
generally has a tail

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:51.790
that's exactly perpendicular.

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:53.820
So this plane and
the plane of the tail

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.100
are perpendicular so
that the tail makes

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:58.170
sure it's always pointing
into the wind, which

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:00.390
is what you want, except
when you're going too fast.

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:03.180
Now you need the tail and
the front of the turbine

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:07.140
to not be perpendicular
but to start to--

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:09.360
hypothetically, if
you're in gales,

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:14.250
you need it to be completely
rotated so that the wind is

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:15.750
just passing by the turbine.

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:18.360
Or if you're in a
moderate wind, where

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:22.920
it's too fast for the
turbine but still usable,

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:24.670
you want it to be
off a little bit.

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:28.980
And so the way this works is
that when the wind speed gets

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:33.300
high enough, you get some
lift in either the tail

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:33.960
or the turbine.

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.570
I forget how it
operates exactly.

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:40.600
And so that allows this
tail stop to be disengaged.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:44.070
So then the turbine
rotates a little bit, which

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:45.723
you can see from this top view.

00:09:45.723 --> 00:09:47.890
These are the front views,
and this is the top view.

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:49.960
So then the turbine
rotates a little bit,

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:51.940
and then the weight
balances out.

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:53.730
And so then the
top engages again,

00:09:53.730 --> 00:09:56.490
and then it maintains
this angle of orientation.

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:59.880
And then if the wind
speed gets even higher,

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:03.600
again, the weights will get out
of balance, things will lift,

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:04.990
the stop will disengage.

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:11.550
So it's a really cool system
to keep your device safe,

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:14.740
and there's a lot
of them out there.

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:20.310
And so I just wanted to show
an example of what happens

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:23.350
when you don't remember--

00:10:23.350 --> 00:10:29.400
where's my mouse-- to include
that or don't do it right.

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:30.630
So watch carefully.

00:10:37.425 --> 00:10:40.770
So that was an expensive,
day and they're

00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:44.910
really lucky that no
one was under there.

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:48.810
So I'll put it back so
you can watch it again.

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:49.902
Can you see?

00:10:49.902 --> 00:10:51.360
Were you saying
couldn't see, Jess?

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:51.720
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:52.760
AMY BANZAERT: Did you
make it work, or--

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:54.460
AUDIENCE: The blade
just fell off.

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:55.252
AMY BANZAERT: What?

00:10:55.252 --> 00:10:56.985
AUDIENCE: Did the
blade just fall off?

00:10:56.985 --> 00:10:58.320
Like, did it just--

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.930
AMY BANZAERT: So
it rotated so fast

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:03.540
that I believe one of
the blades broke off.

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:06.600
And then once one breaks,
it's totally out of balance,

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:09.070
and the whole thing
just falls apart.

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:10.890
And it's moving
at such fast speed

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:14.410
that really crazy
dynamics are going on.

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:16.470
So, yeah, so
there's really solid

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:19.568
reasons to design these
right, especially when

00:11:19.568 --> 00:11:20.610
you're talking big scale.

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:24.580
But even small scale, you
can imagine in a hurricane,

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:28.320
it's not going to be a
safe thing to be near,

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:31.725
and the small ones
are often near people.

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:44.450
OK, so the typical components
of a wind turbine--

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:45.670
you have your rotor.

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:47.920
You have your blades.

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:52.930
You have a brake because you
want the turbine to be turning

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:54.640
at a certain speed, ideally.

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:56.740
And so you may need to
brake, provide braking,

00:11:56.740 --> 00:11:59.140
before you're providing
rotation out of the wind.

00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:03.640
That's another smaller
step you can take.

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:07.240
You have a gearbox to get
the speed to the right speed

00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:09.670
to power your generator.

00:12:09.670 --> 00:12:11.590
Typically, on a
fancier wind turbine,

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:14.980
there's an anemometer that
provides the electrical control

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:19.090
with these yaw drives and
motors to rotate the turbine

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:22.090
into the wind or out
of the wind to maximize

00:12:22.090 --> 00:12:24.820
the speed of your turbine
and then your tower.

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:27.160
And then so all
the electricity is

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:30.790
being generated in
this part of the body,

00:12:30.790 --> 00:12:36.340
and then the power is
just going down the tower.

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:38.890
And you can imagine
the challenge

00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:41.050
of when you need to do
maintenance on this,

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:43.840
and you're talking
about something really

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:45.800
high up that you
have to deal with it.

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:48.790
So the smaller turbines
that we'll be looking at,

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:51.410
they're all on guide
wires and on a pivot

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:52.868
so that you can
rotate them down so

00:12:52.868 --> 00:12:55.243
you don't have to climb up to
do maintenance because that

00:12:55.243 --> 00:12:57.490
requires a much sturdier
tower and is a much more

00:12:57.490 --> 00:13:00.340
dangerous job.

00:13:00.340 --> 00:13:04.150
Some installation considerations
that one would think

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:06.310
would be obvious
but aren't always--

00:13:06.310 --> 00:13:11.650
so this is a wind turbine
that's been installed basically

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:15.550
on a rooftop by some trees.

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:20.950
So it's got two
types of objects that

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:22.570
are blocking the wind entirely.

00:13:22.570 --> 00:13:25.660
So this was a
many-thousand-dollar

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:28.630
installation that's probably
providing zero electricity

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:32.020
because the wind may be
sufficient 30 meters up,

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:34.910
but right there is never
going to be sufficient.

00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:37.090
So the good--

00:13:37.090 --> 00:13:38.530
30 meters, 30 feet.

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:41.950
A good rule of thumb is
you want your turbine

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:46.270
to be 30 feet above any
obstruction within 300 feet.

00:13:46.270 --> 00:13:51.280
And so obstructions include
houses and plants and anything

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:53.380
else that might be in the way.

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:56.590
So next up is vertical-axis
wind turbines,

00:13:56.590 --> 00:14:00.065
and you can see there's
a huge range of them.

00:14:00.065 --> 00:14:02.440
And you probably haven't seen
most of these in production

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:08.860
because, again, they don't have
the same level of efficiencies

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:12.040
and benefits that
horizontal-axis wind

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:13.180
turbines have.

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:15.890
But they're a whole lot
cheaper and easier to build.

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:19.150
So for small-scale solutions,
they can be really ideal.

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:21.860
There's a bunch of different
names of these different types.

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:24.680
So, for example, this is
called a Darrieus wind turbine.

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:26.470
This is a Savonius
wind turbine, which

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:28.610
you'll be building on Friday.

00:14:28.610 --> 00:14:33.450
And this is a cool
combo, Savonius-Darrieus.

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:37.120
The Savonius will start
up automatically in wind,

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:39.340
and the won't but
the Darrieus gets

00:14:39.340 --> 00:14:40.670
a little bit better efficiency.

00:14:40.670 --> 00:14:43.150
So that combining
sort of gets you

00:14:43.150 --> 00:14:46.210
the best of both worlds with
some drawbacks, of course, too.

00:14:46.210 --> 00:14:49.180
And so these power
traffic lights in Taiwan.

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:54.700
So there are places where these
are installed effectively.

00:14:54.700 --> 00:15:00.490
And they're kind of cool
because, as you can see here,

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:02.780
you can almost build
them out of anything,

00:15:02.780 --> 00:15:05.030
and you will build them
out of anything on Friday.

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:06.935
Hey, Amit, do we have
enough plastic bottles,

00:15:06.935 --> 00:15:08.185
speaking of which, for Friday?

00:15:08.185 --> 00:15:09.670
AMIT GANDHI: [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:12.310
AMY BANZAERT: So
added to your homework

00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:14.920
is, on Friday, you need to
bring in a plastic bottle,

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:19.628
like a Gatorade bottle, a
2-liter bottle, whatever.

00:15:19.628 --> 00:15:20.920
It doesn't have to be 2 liters.

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:25.870
It can be 1 liter or
a 20-ounce bottle,

00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:27.100
but we need plastic bottles.

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:29.440
So if everyone brings one,
we'll be in good shape.

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:31.028
Is that doable?

00:15:31.028 --> 00:15:33.070
If you check the recycling
bins walking to class,

00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:33.737
you'll find one.

00:15:33.737 --> 00:15:35.750
[LAUGHS] Cool.

00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:40.130
So some of the advantages,
they're closer to the ground,

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:44.290
so you don't have any
line losses compared

00:15:44.290 --> 00:15:47.440
to the horizontal-axis
wind turbine.

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:49.225
You don't need any
yaw or pitch control

00:15:49.225 --> 00:15:50.600
because they're
just in the wind.

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:54.370
They're intended to be in
the wind, and that works.

00:15:54.370 --> 00:15:57.080
And they work generally
for lower wind speeds,

00:15:57.080 --> 00:15:59.140
which means lower heights.

00:15:59.140 --> 00:16:04.360
They are often much less noisy
than horizontal-axis wind

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:09.190
turbines, and they can
often start at lower speeds.

00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:12.820
But some of the disadvantages,
they're hard to scale up.

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:19.940
So you're never going to get a
7-megawatt vertical-axis wind

00:16:19.940 --> 00:16:20.440
turbine.

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:23.385
That's just not viable.

00:16:23.385 --> 00:16:24.760
They're generally
less efficient,

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:27.100
and they're more
prone to fatigue,

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:30.100
so they do wear out more
easily because they're

00:16:30.100 --> 00:16:33.790
capturing so much more
of the wind, typically.

00:16:33.790 --> 00:16:38.260
But for our purposes,
they're definitely

00:16:38.260 --> 00:16:40.940
a possibility to look at.

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:47.180
For anything, just a
reminder of the equations

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:48.110
we're thinking about--

00:16:48.110 --> 00:16:52.850
so the force that
you're receiving

00:16:52.850 --> 00:16:58.530
is equivalent to 1/2
times rho Av squared.

00:16:58.530 --> 00:17:01.130
So v is obviously the
velocity of the wind,

00:17:01.130 --> 00:17:08.329
the cross-sectional area swept,
and the density of the wind.

00:17:08.329 --> 00:17:14.119
The maximum possible
power extraction

00:17:14.119 --> 00:17:20.760
is dominated by
the velocity cubed.

00:17:20.760 --> 00:17:24.050
So, obviously, velocity is
going to be-- your wind velocity

00:17:24.050 --> 00:17:26.540
is really going to
be what you worry

00:17:26.540 --> 00:17:28.890
about when you're designing.

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:33.600
So area is important, obviously,
but the velocity's cubed,

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:36.047
so the wind speed is huge.

00:17:36.047 --> 00:17:37.880
So whenever you're
thinking, is this a place

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:39.470
where the velocity--

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:41.040
is a wind turbine appropriate?

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:44.870
The first question is,
well, what's the wind speed?

00:17:44.870 --> 00:17:47.490
And then there's a
coefficient of performance,

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:55.410
which just is a reality that
wind turbines are not perfect.

00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:57.870
And so there's a
hypothetical limit.

00:17:57.870 --> 00:17:59.510
And if you google
the best limit,

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:03.710
you can find the proof that's
appealing-- it's kind of cool--

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:07.370
of almost 0.6 is your
hypothetical maximum

00:18:07.370 --> 00:18:08.270
efficiency.

00:18:08.270 --> 00:18:12.900
And then a typical limit in
reality is anywhere from 1/2

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:15.560
for a really sweet
horizontal-axis wind turbine

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:21.170
to less than 1/10 for a
vertical-axis wind turbine

00:18:21.170 --> 00:18:24.950
that I'll show you later
that's really old school

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:28.850
but does the job
when you need it.

00:18:28.850 --> 00:18:31.760
So the other equation that
you want to think about

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:33.540
is the capacity factor.

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:37.640
So I've seen some
websites that say, wind

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:41.584
is so much better than
solar because wind is 24/7.

00:18:41.584 --> 00:18:43.280
Well, not really.

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:46.070
[LAUGHS] Like, unless
you're getting up way

00:18:46.070 --> 00:18:47.720
into the atmosphere--
and even then,

00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:49.520
there's a lot of variation--

00:18:49.520 --> 00:18:50.720
wind is not 24/7.

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:52.800
There's a tremendous
amount of variation.

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:55.730
And so it's generally
20% to 40% of the time

00:18:55.730 --> 00:18:59.810
is how often your
turbine is generating

00:18:59.810 --> 00:19:03.290
power, which is starting
to sound more like solar,

00:19:03.290 --> 00:19:04.790
actually.

00:19:04.790 --> 00:19:07.610
So that's one thing to
definitely keep in mind.

00:19:10.910 --> 00:19:15.150
OK, so a typical power curve--

00:19:15.150 --> 00:19:19.790
so at some point,
at some given speed

00:19:19.790 --> 00:19:22.460
is when the turbine
starts actually turning

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:24.410
because, up until then,
it can't physically

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:26.780
turn because the wind
forces aren't high enough.

00:19:26.780 --> 00:19:32.880
And then your output in terms
of power is very, very low.

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:35.105
And then you have an
average wind speed

00:19:35.105 --> 00:19:36.980
that's generally somewhere
around here, which

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:39.590
is giving you half of the
wind power or the power

00:19:39.590 --> 00:19:40.470
that you'd like.

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:44.810
And then you have a
maximum wind speed,

00:19:44.810 --> 00:19:47.355
and then you're
happy, happy, happy.

00:19:47.355 --> 00:19:48.980
And then at some
point, it gets so high

00:19:48.980 --> 00:19:50.630
that you have to shut
the turbine down,

00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:51.960
or else you're in trouble.

00:19:51.960 --> 00:19:57.130
So that's just a
good general way

00:19:57.130 --> 00:20:00.952
to think about what's happening
over different speeds because--

00:20:00.952 --> 00:20:03.160
I'll show you later, we're
going to look at data that

00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:07.550
shows you the different speeds.

00:20:07.550 --> 00:20:14.770
So these are maps from
NASA that show wind speed

00:20:14.770 --> 00:20:17.680
across the entire globe.

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:21.850
And you can see if it's very
dark, it's very low wind speed.

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:25.430
And if it's very light,
it's higher wind speed.

00:20:25.430 --> 00:20:30.040
So if we could just fill
this part of the ocean

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:32.590
with wind turbines, that
would be pretty sweet.

00:20:32.590 --> 00:20:35.690
But you can actually
see in Nicaragua,

00:20:35.690 --> 00:20:40.330
which is right over here, you're
getting pretty decent wind.

00:20:40.330 --> 00:20:42.040
Compared to, say,
if we were working

00:20:42.040 --> 00:20:46.240
in these parts of Africa or up
here in Russia or something,

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:49.070
we wouldn't be thinking
wind turbines at all.

00:20:49.070 --> 00:20:50.890
And you can see how
much variation there

00:20:50.890 --> 00:20:52.168
is between January and July.

00:20:52.168 --> 00:20:54.460
And so, of course, there's
a lot of seasonal variation,

00:20:54.460 --> 00:20:56.260
no matter what.

00:20:56.260 --> 00:20:59.540
And you can see this blue
is 7 meters per second,

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:02.200
which is getting to be useful.

00:21:02.200 --> 00:21:03.910
For a horizontal-axis
wind turbine,

00:21:03.910 --> 00:21:06.280
you need 5 meters per second.

00:21:06.280 --> 00:21:08.500
For vertical, you can
go a little bit lower,

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:12.860
but 5 is a good rule of thumb.

00:21:12.860 --> 00:21:16.300
And again, why we're looking at
velocity over everything else

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:20.900
is because velocity is cubed
for your power equation.

00:21:20.900 --> 00:21:23.380
So great, I've told
you wind speeds.

00:21:23.380 --> 00:21:26.170
I've showed you a NASA
map of the entire Earth,

00:21:26.170 --> 00:21:29.330
but that doesn't tell you
much for a given location.

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:33.460
So one way to get a really
rough estimate of wind speed

00:21:33.460 --> 00:21:37.780
is this Beaufort scale, which
was developed in the 1800s.

00:21:37.780 --> 00:21:40.510
And it correlates what
you can visually see

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:43.760
and what kind of damage
you're seeing to the speed.

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:47.710
Some of the caveats on this are
number one, it's pretty rough.

00:21:47.710 --> 00:21:49.990
And especially if
you're talking to people

00:21:49.990 --> 00:21:52.510
in a community saying,
tell me about wind speed,

00:21:52.510 --> 00:21:55.280
and giving them examples,
it's hard to remember

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:56.530
exactly what the wind is like.

00:21:56.530 --> 00:21:59.650
Like, can you tell me what
the Beaufort scale was today

00:21:59.650 --> 00:22:02.500
walking here?

00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:03.130
I can't.

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:04.840
[LAUGHS] I don't remember.

00:22:04.840 --> 00:22:07.360
I don't really pay attention
to the wind that much.

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:08.470
If you're working
with farmers, they're

00:22:08.470 --> 00:22:11.095
probably a little bit more aware
of it than you and I might be,

00:22:11.095 --> 00:22:12.820
but still, it's challenging.

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:15.220
And the other thing is
that a lot of these metrics

00:22:15.220 --> 00:22:17.530
are on the ground,
and we're probably

00:22:17.530 --> 00:22:19.690
talking about significantly
higher than the ground,

00:22:19.690 --> 00:22:22.160
depending on what kind of
turbine you're implementing.

00:22:22.160 --> 00:22:24.860
And so again, that
only helps you.

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:27.250
So if you're finding
out that you're probably

00:22:27.250 --> 00:22:33.580
at 4 miles an hour, which is
2 meters per second and really

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:35.740
low, but at the
very ground level,

00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:39.710
and you're building a
turbine that's 30 feet up,

00:22:39.710 --> 00:22:42.550
maybe you have a shot
of getting enough wind.

00:22:42.550 --> 00:22:43.420
Yeah.

00:22:43.420 --> 00:22:44.753
AUDIENCE: Just a quick question.

00:22:44.753 --> 00:22:47.638
Is there a uniform
scale from what

00:22:47.638 --> 00:22:49.180
the wind speed at
the ground level is

00:22:49.180 --> 00:22:52.330
and how it changes vertically?

00:22:52.330 --> 00:22:54.080
Or is that a regional
thing like you said?

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:55.455
AMY BANZAERT:
Well, so it's both.

00:22:55.455 --> 00:22:58.420
So yes, there are
some scales that

00:22:58.420 --> 00:23:00.820
can help you estimate, if
you know what the ground is,

00:23:00.820 --> 00:23:01.870
what it is up above.

00:23:01.870 --> 00:23:04.580
But it also is really
dependent on what's around.

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:07.240
And so I'm about to
show you a tool that

00:23:07.240 --> 00:23:10.840
helps a little bit with that,
but it's still very imperfect.

00:23:10.840 --> 00:23:12.940
Ideally, you'd
have an anemometer,

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:15.130
and you leave it
up for five years,

00:23:15.130 --> 00:23:17.980
and you'd have it at 10
different heights, 10

00:23:17.980 --> 00:23:20.350
different anemometers, 10
different heights, and that

00:23:20.350 --> 00:23:23.710
would give you actual real
data that you'd be psyched for.

00:23:23.710 --> 00:23:26.960
But that would be a
really ridiculously slow,

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:28.060
expensive project.

00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:29.950
And so a lot of this
is just estimations

00:23:29.950 --> 00:23:32.530
and doing your best guesses.

00:23:32.530 --> 00:23:35.650
So this, I'm sorry
for the web link.

00:23:35.650 --> 00:23:37.900
As you'll see, this
website is web--

00:23:37.900 --> 00:23:39.160
it's not even web 1.0.

00:23:39.160 --> 00:23:40.720
It's like web 0.5.

00:23:40.720 --> 00:23:44.770
But it has awesome
data from NASA.

00:23:44.770 --> 00:23:50.970
So you go to this website
with that awful link,

00:23:50.970 --> 00:23:53.340
and then there's a
bunch of different ways

00:23:53.340 --> 00:23:54.300
to get this data.

00:23:54.300 --> 00:23:57.190
And this is actually both
useful for solar and wind.

00:23:57.190 --> 00:23:59.190
So if you're worrying
about solar data too,

00:23:59.190 --> 00:24:00.160
this is really useful.

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:04.050
So if you don't know your
latitude and longitude,

00:24:04.050 --> 00:24:07.390
you can click, use
the map to click.

00:24:07.390 --> 00:24:10.200
Every time you do this,
you have to log in,

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:13.920
but it's very easy to
join because you just

00:24:13.920 --> 00:24:16.740
type your password twice instead
of once you become a member.

00:24:20.670 --> 00:24:27.570
And then it's slow,
but it looks so clunky

00:24:27.570 --> 00:24:29.430
that I was afraid you'd
be turned off by it.

00:24:29.430 --> 00:24:31.138
And it has good enough
data that I wanted

00:24:31.138 --> 00:24:34.090
to go through showing you it.

00:24:34.090 --> 00:24:36.840
So you have this
map of the Earth.

00:24:36.840 --> 00:24:38.790
You click in the
part of the Earth

00:24:38.790 --> 00:24:42.900
that you care
about to center it.

00:24:42.900 --> 00:24:44.115
You click to zoom.

00:24:47.430 --> 00:24:53.190
We're in the wrong part of the
world, but we're getting there.

00:24:53.190 --> 00:24:54.615
I'm going to zoom in again.

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:00.810
And you can see like this
is data for a region,

00:25:00.810 --> 00:25:02.490
not for a small town.

00:25:02.490 --> 00:25:06.280
So there's all sorts of limits
to this data, but it's useful.

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:09.210
And so now you can
finally get your data,

00:25:09.210 --> 00:25:12.210
and now you can get
crazy information--

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:18.180
so parameters for solar
cooking, for solar panels,

00:25:18.180 --> 00:25:21.300
cloud information, which
can be really useful,

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:24.430
temperature, which
can be really useful.

00:25:24.430 --> 00:25:25.690
And now we're getting to wind.

00:25:25.690 --> 00:25:27.780
So you can see the variety.

00:25:27.780 --> 00:25:30.090
So you can get 50
meter and 10 meter,

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:33.270
but because this is all
satellite data or airport

00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:34.410
data--

00:25:34.410 --> 00:25:37.660
so 50 meters is pretty high for
anything we're talking about,

00:25:37.660 --> 00:25:40.620
and then 10 meters for
terrain similar to the airport

00:25:40.620 --> 00:25:42.862
because they're
measuring at the airport

00:25:42.862 --> 00:25:45.070
because that's the place
where they have anemometers.

00:25:45.070 --> 00:25:46.800
So all of this data,
again, you have

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:48.690
to take with a grain
of salt, but it's

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:50.580
good to-- if you use
that in conjunction

00:25:50.580 --> 00:25:52.470
with your own visual
observations in talking

00:25:52.470 --> 00:25:55.320
to people, it's better
than nothing, which

00:25:55.320 --> 00:25:58.830
is the alternative for a
million-dollar 10-year study,

00:25:58.830 --> 00:26:01.510
which isn't viable either.

00:26:01.510 --> 00:26:03.570
So we're going to just
look at wind speed

00:26:03.570 --> 00:26:10.765
at 50 meters for that area
of Nicaragua I just selected.

00:26:16.610 --> 00:26:20.470
And you can see
a 10-year average

00:26:20.470 --> 00:26:23.900
and a minimum and
maximum variation.

00:26:23.900 --> 00:26:28.660
So it's pretty helpful just to
get you into the ballpark of,

00:26:28.660 --> 00:26:30.980
do I have a shot of
thinking this might work?

00:26:30.980 --> 00:26:35.470
And then some of the other
things that you can select for

00:26:35.470 --> 00:26:40.270
are how high you are--

00:26:40.270 --> 00:26:43.060
so, Jessica, this is getting to
what you were interested in--

00:26:43.060 --> 00:26:46.810
like, an estimate of where you
are and what your ground cover

00:26:46.810 --> 00:26:50.090
is, and then it extrapolates
what the wind will be.

00:26:50.090 --> 00:26:53.410
So it's pretty
useful and powerful.

00:26:53.410 --> 00:26:57.040
Is it 100% accurate,
given that it's doing

00:26:57.040 --> 00:26:59.030
really broad-based readings?

00:26:59.030 --> 00:27:02.080
Of course not, but it's a start.

00:27:02.080 --> 00:27:08.900
So it's a nice
resource to know about.

00:27:08.900 --> 00:27:10.600
So now I want to
spend a little time

00:27:10.600 --> 00:27:15.350
to talk about examples of wind
power in developing countries.

00:27:15.350 --> 00:27:18.880
So one is the
Humdinger Windbelt,

00:27:18.880 --> 00:27:21.500
which is totally different
from what you're used to seeing

00:27:21.500 --> 00:27:23.500
and was developed by a
student who actually took

00:27:23.500 --> 00:27:26.980
one of the first D-Lab
classes back when

00:27:26.980 --> 00:27:28.960
it was the Haiti class.

00:27:28.960 --> 00:27:31.510
And we have a Windbelt here,
but I couldn't find it,

00:27:31.510 --> 00:27:34.420
so maybe during break,
I'll be able to dig it up.

00:27:34.420 --> 00:27:37.270
Or if not, maybe we
can bring it by Friday

00:27:37.270 --> 00:27:40.010
and show it in lab on Friday.

00:27:40.010 --> 00:27:45.040
So the way the Windbelt works
is that instead of a rotation,

00:27:45.040 --> 00:27:50.290
it just uses the vibration of
the belt, very similar to--

00:27:50.290 --> 00:27:55.450
I don't if you guys
can remember the name

00:27:55.450 --> 00:27:58.350
of the bridge that collapsed.

00:27:58.350 --> 00:27:59.350
AUDIENCE: Tacoma Narrow.

00:27:59.350 --> 00:28:00.190
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:28:00.190 --> 00:28:02.230
Does everyone know that
Tacoma Narrows Bridge?

00:28:02.230 --> 00:28:05.500
Yeah, so, well, google it
because you can see videos,

00:28:05.500 --> 00:28:07.310
and it's awesome if you haven't.

00:28:07.310 --> 00:28:12.115
So, yeah, it is totally
scary, especially because--

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:16.730
I have a friend who did
some research into it.

00:28:16.730 --> 00:28:21.130
And so this bridge was
a super long bridge,

00:28:21.130 --> 00:28:26.770
and it just had natural
frequencies at the same rate

00:28:26.770 --> 00:28:28.990
as the wind was often blowing.

00:28:28.990 --> 00:28:31.990
And so the bridge would
just go up and down

00:28:31.990 --> 00:28:33.880
and up and down crazily.

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:36.220
And eventually, one
day, it got so bad

00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:38.020
that it completely broke apart.

00:28:38.020 --> 00:28:40.150
And the crazy thing
is that people

00:28:40.150 --> 00:28:42.460
used to hang out on the bridge
on the really windy days

00:28:42.460 --> 00:28:44.020
because it was so fun.

00:28:44.020 --> 00:28:45.540
It was like a roller coaster.

00:28:45.540 --> 00:28:47.880
And so it's extra scary
thinking about that.

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:51.010
So anyway, but this
is the inspiration

00:28:51.010 --> 00:28:54.880
for the Humdinger, which just
has a really tightly pulled

00:28:54.880 --> 00:28:59.830
belt in the middle of a
support structure so that the--

00:28:59.830 --> 00:29:02.290
and you put this wind, so the
wind is going perpendicular

00:29:02.290 --> 00:29:03.200
to the board.

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:06.160
And so that hits the natural
frequencies of the belt

00:29:06.160 --> 00:29:07.450
and causes it to vibrate.

00:29:07.450 --> 00:29:11.410
And then you just have
some magnets next to a coil

00:29:11.410 --> 00:29:14.500
on the corner that allow
you to generate electricity.

00:29:14.500 --> 00:29:18.430
So for certain
applications, this

00:29:18.430 --> 00:29:23.170
can be a really low-cost
way to provide electricity.

00:29:23.170 --> 00:29:26.835
And this is a pretty
relatively fancy way

00:29:26.835 --> 00:29:28.960
of building it, but you
can imagine, and they have,

00:29:28.960 --> 00:29:31.540
building it out of
like a PVC pipe,

00:29:31.540 --> 00:29:35.230
which you can get anywhere,
and building it really simply.

00:29:35.230 --> 00:29:38.500
And because there's very
few moving parts and nothing

00:29:38.500 --> 00:29:42.160
rotating, it's much
easier to build than even

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:44.500
a vertical-axis wind turbine.

00:29:44.500 --> 00:29:46.570
And so it's a really
neat application.

00:29:46.570 --> 00:29:49.510
And hypothetically, you
could imagine building it

00:29:49.510 --> 00:29:55.120
into structures like railings
on buildings and stuff

00:29:55.120 --> 00:29:58.640
in order to power
different things.

00:29:58.640 --> 00:29:59.680
So this is one example.

00:29:59.680 --> 00:30:02.830
And we do have at
least a couple D-Labs,

00:30:02.830 --> 00:30:07.750
so we'll make sure to show you
them later today or tomorrow.

00:30:07.750 --> 00:30:13.240
Another example is the
classic wind turbine

00:30:13.240 --> 00:30:17.300
in developing countries was
designed by Hugh Piggott.

00:30:17.300 --> 00:30:23.620
And these are pictures of work
being done at blueEnergy, which

00:30:23.620 --> 00:30:25.930
is in Bluefields in Nicaragua,
and we worked with them

00:30:25.930 --> 00:30:30.730
last year, and they
build all these turbines.

00:30:30.730 --> 00:30:33.370
So you can see there's
the support wires

00:30:33.370 --> 00:30:37.720
and that you can rotate the
turbine down so that you

00:30:37.720 --> 00:30:41.770
can do maintenance on it,
which is a really nice feature.

00:30:41.770 --> 00:30:44.890
So that's one
classic design that's

00:30:44.890 --> 00:30:47.590
pretty common in
developing countries.

00:30:47.590 --> 00:30:51.190
And then a lot of people have
heard of William Kamkwamba.

00:30:51.190 --> 00:30:54.700
Oh, there's the Windbelt,
so let me go back to that.

00:30:54.700 --> 00:30:58.510
So I'll pass this around,
and the belt is not

00:30:58.510 --> 00:31:00.940
exactly taught at the moment.

00:31:00.940 --> 00:31:06.820
But you can see just the belt,
the magnet on the belt that

00:31:06.820 --> 00:31:08.628
goes against the
coil, and that's

00:31:08.628 --> 00:31:11.170
really all there is to it, and
then a little bit of circuitry

00:31:11.170 --> 00:31:14.110
in order to generate power,
which is a really simple way

00:31:14.110 --> 00:31:14.732
to do things.

00:31:14.732 --> 00:31:15.940
AUDIENCE: So what's the idea?

00:31:15.940 --> 00:31:19.050
The wind blows through
the tape there?

00:31:19.050 --> 00:31:21.060
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:31:21.060 --> 00:31:23.782
And the tape vibrates just
its natural frequencies,

00:31:23.782 --> 00:31:25.990
and the vibration allows
you to generate electricity.

00:31:25.990 --> 00:31:28.370
It's movement is all you need.

00:31:32.620 --> 00:31:33.120
Thanks.

00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:34.740
[LAUGHS]

00:31:34.740 --> 00:31:39.780
So William Kamkwamba
was this guy--

00:31:39.780 --> 00:31:41.695
where did he live?

00:31:41.695 --> 00:31:42.570
Did I write it down--

00:31:45.340 --> 00:31:49.810
in Malawi, and he just got
really interested in how

00:31:49.810 --> 00:31:53.500
he could get electricity at his
house, and went to the library,

00:31:53.500 --> 00:31:55.540
and read a bunch of
books, and got inspired,

00:31:55.540 --> 00:31:59.800
and built his own
wind turbine, which

00:31:59.800 --> 00:32:03.280
doesn't look like your
traditional wind turbine.

00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:04.840
There are a few
design improvements

00:32:04.840 --> 00:32:08.050
that could be made, especially
with better resources.

00:32:08.050 --> 00:32:11.470
But worked well enough
for him to power

00:32:11.470 --> 00:32:14.200
a radial at his house
and charge cell phones,

00:32:14.200 --> 00:32:18.040
which got people really excited
so that they would start

00:32:18.040 --> 00:32:20.410
paying him, so he
could start building

00:32:20.410 --> 00:32:23.410
more and more turbines.

00:32:23.410 --> 00:32:29.260
So it's a really cool story,
and now he's at Dartmouth,

00:32:29.260 --> 00:32:32.950
in school, which he'd never
could have done had he not

00:32:32.950 --> 00:32:33.880
built this turbine.

00:32:33.880 --> 00:32:35.470
So he's a cool guy.

00:32:35.470 --> 00:32:39.190
We may be able to get him to
come down to talk to our class

00:32:39.190 --> 00:32:39.790
this year.

00:32:39.790 --> 00:32:40.510
We'll see.

00:32:40.510 --> 00:32:41.380
I'm trying.

00:32:41.380 --> 00:32:43.930
But his advisor at
Dartmouth is very reluctant

00:32:43.930 --> 00:32:45.025
to let him travel.

00:32:45.025 --> 00:32:46.870
[LAUGHS] I'm sorry?

00:32:46.870 --> 00:32:47.980
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:47.980 --> 00:32:49.720
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:32:49.720 --> 00:32:51.760
But because he
didn't grow up here,

00:32:51.760 --> 00:32:56.140
he's getting very parented
at a different level

00:32:56.140 --> 00:33:02.990
than you guys are by the
professor at Dartmouth.

00:33:02.990 --> 00:33:07.540
So anyway, so just a cool
example of something very low

00:33:07.540 --> 00:33:10.690
tech that worked
really well and made

00:33:10.690 --> 00:33:16.060
a remarkable change for this
guy and also his village.

00:33:16.060 --> 00:33:17.800
And then working
all the way down

00:33:17.800 --> 00:33:22.030
to the really simplest wind
turbines, so this is a--

00:33:22.030 --> 00:33:24.640
I don't if you say it
as "pah-neh-mo-nee"

00:33:24.640 --> 00:33:27.670
or "pa-neh-mone,"
but one of those--

00:33:27.670 --> 00:33:32.050
wind turbine, which is
incredibly old technology.

00:33:32.050 --> 00:33:35.900
This is the 10,000-BC technology
that we're talking about,

00:33:35.900 --> 00:33:38.380
and it's just sails that rotate.

00:33:38.380 --> 00:33:41.680
The sails-- actually, each
one rotates 180 degrees, So.

00:33:41.680 --> 00:33:44.680
They're always
moving in the wind.

00:33:44.680 --> 00:33:46.370
And this is not a turbine.

00:33:46.370 --> 00:33:47.650
This is a mill.

00:33:47.650 --> 00:33:51.898
So the act of it spinning
pulls this rope pump.

00:33:51.898 --> 00:33:54.190
Maybe if I turn off the
lights, it'll be easier to see.

00:34:03.660 --> 00:34:06.500
So there's a rope pump,
here, and the way a rope pump

00:34:06.500 --> 00:34:08.460
works is incredibly simple.

00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:16.659
You just have a rope inside
a tube that goes down

00:34:16.659 --> 00:34:26.540
into the water table so
that you're getting water

00:34:26.540 --> 00:34:28.400
through holes in the tube.

00:34:28.400 --> 00:34:30.920
And then you either
have knots in the rope

00:34:30.920 --> 00:34:34.550
or, if you can find
them, rubber washers

00:34:34.550 --> 00:34:36.170
every foot along the tube.

00:34:36.170 --> 00:34:39.870
And this is obviously
not to scale in any way.

00:34:39.870 --> 00:34:44.360
And so the water gets
trapped between the washers,

00:34:44.360 --> 00:34:46.380
and you pull up
the water that way.

00:34:46.380 --> 00:34:48.230
And so this is really simple.

00:34:48.230 --> 00:34:51.080
Again, we're talking about
ridiculously low efficiencies.

00:34:51.080 --> 00:34:52.760
But if you need
to irrigate, this

00:34:52.760 --> 00:34:56.600
is a way to do it that just
uses the wind that works really

00:34:56.600 --> 00:34:58.010
well.

00:34:58.010 --> 00:35:01.340
And this was built
for $40, which

00:35:01.340 --> 00:35:03.590
is ridiculously inexpensive
compared to even

00:35:03.590 --> 00:35:05.300
like a Hugh Piggott
wind turbine, which

00:35:05.300 --> 00:35:07.830
is in the thousands of dollars.

00:35:07.830 --> 00:35:13.790
So there are opportunities for
a whole range of possibilities.

00:35:13.790 --> 00:35:17.190
And then, yeah, there's just so
many different types out there.

00:35:17.190 --> 00:35:19.367
So if you look into
it, wind is one

00:35:19.367 --> 00:35:21.200
of the areas where
people get really excited

00:35:21.200 --> 00:35:22.617
and like to build
their own stuff.

00:35:22.617 --> 00:35:24.320
And so you can
see a huge variety

00:35:24.320 --> 00:35:28.020
of designs in the world.

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:30.830
So that's wind in a
very fast nutshell.

00:35:30.830 --> 00:35:32.630
Are there any
questions about that?

00:35:38.870 --> 00:35:43.700
OK, so next is micro hydro.

00:35:43.700 --> 00:35:46.670
And micro hydro is kind of
funny because it's harder

00:35:46.670 --> 00:35:49.700
to find really good examples
of installations the developing

00:35:49.700 --> 00:35:54.080
world because they tend to
be just built but not well

00:35:54.080 --> 00:35:57.110
documented is what I'm finding.

00:35:57.110 --> 00:35:58.820
So I'm going to
talk more about how

00:35:58.820 --> 00:36:03.650
you would determine if a
micro hydro installation would

00:36:03.650 --> 00:36:04.370
be appropriate.

00:36:04.370 --> 00:36:06.800
And I don't have as many good
examples of installations

00:36:06.800 --> 00:36:11.600
that are successful, but
they're certainly out there.

00:36:11.600 --> 00:36:15.560
So hydropower is just using
the gravitational flow of water

00:36:15.560 --> 00:36:19.070
to generate power,
considering both--

00:36:19.070 --> 00:36:24.840
the two terms I'm going to
use are head and flow rate.

00:36:24.840 --> 00:36:28.310
So head is the distance that the
water falls, and then flow rate

00:36:28.310 --> 00:36:30.990
is how quickly the
water is moving.

00:36:30.990 --> 00:36:36.110
And so hydropower can be at
2 megawatts and much beyond

00:36:36.110 --> 00:36:42.740
and is a sizable impact on our
nation's electricity supply,

00:36:42.740 --> 00:36:48.560
for example, all the way down to
a 200-watt turbine or smaller.

00:36:48.560 --> 00:36:51.437
So I'm saying micro
hydro for this lecture.

00:36:51.437 --> 00:36:53.270
Probably some of the
stuff I'm talking about

00:36:53.270 --> 00:36:57.595
is actually pico
or mini ranging.

00:36:57.595 --> 00:36:59.220
And everyone defines
these differently,

00:36:59.220 --> 00:37:01.580
so if you look up
what pico hydro is,

00:37:01.580 --> 00:37:03.150
you'll see 10
different definitions.

00:37:03.150 --> 00:37:06.260
So it's not critical that
you're getting exactly,

00:37:06.260 --> 00:37:11.350
and you can even
see between pico

00:37:11.350 --> 00:37:15.820
and micro, there's something
according to this definition.

00:37:20.360 --> 00:37:25.970
Yeah, and with water
power, it's the same

00:37:25.970 --> 00:37:28.490
as wind in terms of there's
mills and there's turbines.

00:37:28.490 --> 00:37:32.180
So you can think of the old
grist mills and other things

00:37:32.180 --> 00:37:34.730
that you can see in parts
of New England still.

00:37:37.820 --> 00:37:40.310
Again, they're low
velocity, high torque,

00:37:40.310 --> 00:37:44.900
delivering mechanical
power directly,

00:37:44.900 --> 00:37:48.320
as opposed to a turbine,
which is much higher

00:37:48.320 --> 00:37:50.840
velocity, much lower
torque to allow

00:37:50.840 --> 00:37:52.440
you to generate electricity.

00:37:52.440 --> 00:37:55.110
So those are the two extremes.

00:37:55.110 --> 00:37:55.610
Yeah.

00:37:58.750 --> 00:38:02.210
In terms of hydropower,
there's two methods.

00:38:02.210 --> 00:38:04.840
So one is called
run of the river,

00:38:04.840 --> 00:38:10.090
and that means that you're just
taking a chunk of the river

00:38:10.090 --> 00:38:13.180
and siphoning it
off into a tube that

00:38:13.180 --> 00:38:18.130
goes directly into your turbine,
and the other option is a dam.

00:38:18.130 --> 00:38:21.350
So the benefits of this are
that it's relatively simple.

00:38:21.350 --> 00:38:23.590
You can build it
locally, compared

00:38:23.590 --> 00:38:27.070
to a dam is much harder
to build generally.

00:38:27.070 --> 00:38:29.740
It's comparatively much
lower environmental impact

00:38:29.740 --> 00:38:31.270
than a dam.

00:38:31.270 --> 00:38:33.340
They're relatively affordable.

00:38:33.340 --> 00:38:36.833
But when you have a drought,
you don't have any storage

00:38:36.833 --> 00:38:38.875
like you have with a dam,
so that's the drawback.

00:38:42.190 --> 00:38:46.390
And the dam is the reverse,
that you can accumulate

00:38:46.390 --> 00:38:49.610
your rainfall, which is great,
but it's relatively expensive,

00:38:49.610 --> 00:38:50.110
And.

00:38:50.110 --> 00:38:52.480
There's a greater
environmental impacts

00:38:52.480 --> 00:38:55.990
on the negative
because damming up

00:38:55.990 --> 00:38:58.510
water affects the
local environment,

00:38:58.510 --> 00:39:02.530
from the fish to just what the
fields look like, et cetera, et

00:39:02.530 --> 00:39:04.040
cetera.

00:39:04.040 --> 00:39:05.950
I'm sure you've heard
of dams in China

00:39:05.950 --> 00:39:12.880
are similar that have destroyed
entire communities for building

00:39:12.880 --> 00:39:14.995
dams on a very,
very large scale.

00:39:19.693 --> 00:39:21.110
Yeah, and there's
more maintenance

00:39:21.110 --> 00:39:23.930
associated with the dam, too,
in terms of silt accumulation.

00:39:23.930 --> 00:39:25.400
It's challenging.

00:39:25.400 --> 00:39:27.650
So we're just going to talk
about the run of the river

00:39:27.650 --> 00:39:28.650
model.

00:39:28.650 --> 00:39:32.540
So the components that
you have is something

00:39:32.540 --> 00:39:35.930
called a weir that diverts
a chunk of the water

00:39:35.930 --> 00:39:38.360
into your tube.

00:39:38.360 --> 00:39:40.010
You generally have
a settling basin

00:39:40.010 --> 00:39:43.868
so that any debris
settles out because you

00:39:43.868 --> 00:39:45.410
don't want it going
into your turbine

00:39:45.410 --> 00:39:47.510
because you'll
destroy your turbine.

00:39:47.510 --> 00:39:50.000
A forebay, which
is a small holding

00:39:50.000 --> 00:39:52.610
tank-- so you're getting
reserve capacity that way,

00:39:52.610 --> 00:39:54.740
but that's talking
about variation

00:39:54.740 --> 00:40:00.380
on the order of seconds or
minutes, not days or months.

00:40:00.380 --> 00:40:04.940
Penstock, which is a closed
pipe flowing downhill--

00:40:04.940 --> 00:40:09.530
that feeds directly into your
turbine that then generates

00:40:09.530 --> 00:40:11.510
your electricity,
and then the water

00:40:11.510 --> 00:40:14.810
is returned back to the river.

00:40:14.810 --> 00:40:17.590
There's a lot of
different turbine types.

00:40:17.590 --> 00:40:20.780
So one of the most efficient,
if not the most efficient,

00:40:20.780 --> 00:40:22.190
is called a Pelton wheel.

00:40:22.190 --> 00:40:26.430
And you can see it's got a
relatively complex geometry,

00:40:26.430 --> 00:40:30.050
and so relatively expensive
and challenging to build.

00:40:30.050 --> 00:40:33.750
But if you do, it's
very efficient.

00:40:33.750 --> 00:40:37.530
It works well for a
high-head, low-flow situation.

00:40:37.530 --> 00:40:42.290
So again, head is how far
the water is dropping.

00:40:42.290 --> 00:40:44.570
Francis wheels,
which work better

00:40:44.570 --> 00:40:49.640
for lower head, higher flow,
which has a spiral casing where

00:40:49.640 --> 00:40:52.790
the water comes in and
spins this turbine and then

00:40:52.790 --> 00:40:53.375
comes out.

00:40:57.020 --> 00:41:02.113
Cross-flow, where the water
flows over this cylinder,

00:41:02.113 --> 00:41:03.530
which has a bunch
of blades on it,

00:41:03.530 --> 00:41:09.320
and that's what spins the
shaft to generate electricity.

00:41:09.320 --> 00:41:12.770
A propeller, which literally
uses a ship's propeller,

00:41:12.770 --> 00:41:15.680
and instead of the ship's
propeller being powered

00:41:15.680 --> 00:41:18.080
in order to move the
boat, it's just using it

00:41:18.080 --> 00:41:20.780
in the exact opposite way.

00:41:20.780 --> 00:41:24.950
And you can see that you need
different types of turbines

00:41:24.950 --> 00:41:28.160
for different flow
and head situations.

00:41:28.160 --> 00:41:32.000
And then one example
is just literally

00:41:32.000 --> 00:41:37.280
putting something in the river,
skipping all that extra stuff

00:41:37.280 --> 00:41:40.280
of siphoning off the
water and just putting

00:41:40.280 --> 00:41:42.350
something on top
of the water, which

00:41:42.350 --> 00:41:45.650
seems really simple and great
until you think about all

00:41:45.650 --> 00:41:47.540
the challenges in
terms of flooding,

00:41:47.540 --> 00:41:50.640
and debris in the water,
and et cetera, et cetera.

00:41:50.640 --> 00:41:58.940
This is a device that is
really prone to failure,

00:41:58.940 --> 00:42:03.050
but it works in areas where
that's your only option,

00:42:03.050 --> 00:42:05.360
or it can work.

00:42:05.360 --> 00:42:07.280
Yeah.

00:42:07.280 --> 00:42:10.850
So some of the considerations
you need for micro hydro--

00:42:10.850 --> 00:42:20.240
one is that the minimum head and
flow you need is 3 feet of head

00:42:20.240 --> 00:42:23.210
and 20 gallons per
minute of flow.

00:42:23.210 --> 00:42:26.130
So that would be the
minimum head you need.

00:42:26.130 --> 00:42:32.330
And if you have more
head, either, bonus,

00:42:32.330 --> 00:42:35.330
you get more power,
or you can therefore

00:42:35.330 --> 00:42:40.190
have reduced flow because the
power equation here is, again,

00:42:40.190 --> 00:42:43.250
a coefficient of
efficiency times

00:42:43.250 --> 00:42:45.770
the water density
and gravity times

00:42:45.770 --> 00:42:49.140
the height, which is your head,
and Q, which is your flow rate.

00:42:49.140 --> 00:42:53.480
So you can see that hQ,
you can keep constant.

00:42:53.480 --> 00:42:56.480
If you increase your head,
you can lower your velocity,

00:42:56.480 --> 00:42:57.890
and keep your power constant.

00:43:00.590 --> 00:43:04.340
One nice just rule of thumb
is if you stick with SI units,

00:43:04.340 --> 00:43:06.050
rho g is about 10.

00:43:06.050 --> 00:43:08.570
So you can just plug that
in rather than having

00:43:08.570 --> 00:43:10.820
to remember the density
of water and gravity,

00:43:10.820 --> 00:43:12.890
though in SI units,
that's relatively easy,

00:43:12.890 --> 00:43:19.250
but 10 is a nice way
to just remember it.

00:43:19.250 --> 00:43:22.970
And then efficiency is somewhere
generally between 40% and 70%.

00:43:26.010 --> 00:43:28.050
One other thing to
think about is just,

00:43:28.050 --> 00:43:31.670
when you have your penstock--

00:43:31.670 --> 00:43:33.420
so that's the water
going through the pipe

00:43:33.420 --> 00:43:36.810
to get from the top of the
river down to your generator--

00:43:36.810 --> 00:43:39.438
if your pipe diameter
is too small,

00:43:39.438 --> 00:43:41.730
you're going to have frictional
losses in the pipe that

00:43:41.730 --> 00:43:43.680
dominate and slow
your water down

00:43:43.680 --> 00:43:45.400
so much that it's problematic.

00:43:45.400 --> 00:43:47.610
So that's another
thing to remember

00:43:47.610 --> 00:43:50.820
because it's tempting to
use small-diameter pipe

00:43:50.820 --> 00:43:53.010
for a variety of reasons,
but that's dangerous.

00:43:55.800 --> 00:44:03.120
So how do you figure out if a
river is appropriate for using

00:44:03.120 --> 00:44:04.020
micro hydro?

00:44:04.020 --> 00:44:07.230
So the two things you need to
know, again, are flow and head.

00:44:07.230 --> 00:44:11.940
So the simplest method I
can find for measuring flow

00:44:11.940 --> 00:44:13.650
is called a float method.

00:44:13.650 --> 00:44:16.380
And you can imagine actually
doing this in a river

00:44:16.380 --> 00:44:18.480
would be challenging,
so it's not trivial,

00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:20.790
but it is possible.

00:44:20.790 --> 00:44:24.690
So you mark about 100
feet along a length

00:44:24.690 --> 00:44:29.940
that's ideally straight with
uniform width and depth, which

00:44:29.940 --> 00:44:33.150
is comical to find
in a river, right?

00:44:33.150 --> 00:44:35.250
Like, I've never
found a river that

00:44:35.250 --> 00:44:37.860
has 100 feet of uniform
width and depth,

00:44:37.860 --> 00:44:39.900
but you do your best.

00:44:39.900 --> 00:44:42.960
And then you calculate your
cross-sectional area A. People

00:44:42.960 --> 00:44:48.840
usually model it as a
semicircle, or a trapezoid,

00:44:48.840 --> 00:44:53.190
or even just a
rectangle, calculate

00:44:53.190 --> 00:44:54.810
that cross-sectional area.

00:44:54.810 --> 00:44:58.500
You place a marker at the
start and end of your 100 feet,

00:44:58.500 --> 00:45:00.030
and then you put
floats in the river

00:45:00.030 --> 00:45:04.530
at the center of the stream
and time them to find out

00:45:04.530 --> 00:45:07.620
how long it takes for them to
get from one end to the other.

00:45:07.620 --> 00:45:09.532
If you want to get
complicated, you

00:45:09.532 --> 00:45:12.630
can put them all along a
bunch of different points

00:45:12.630 --> 00:45:15.810
at different levels to
worry about friction losses,

00:45:15.810 --> 00:45:18.430
but you don't actually have
to do that to get a ballpark

00:45:18.430 --> 00:45:18.930
measurement.

00:45:18.930 --> 00:45:21.460
You can just do
it in the center.

00:45:21.460 --> 00:45:24.660
And so good floats
could be a tennis ball,

00:45:24.660 --> 00:45:27.570
could be an apple, or
something like that, whatever

00:45:27.570 --> 00:45:29.640
you can find that floats.

00:45:29.640 --> 00:45:32.340
And then you do have to
use a conversion factor

00:45:32.340 --> 00:45:36.540
because your water is flowing
faster at the top of the water

00:45:36.540 --> 00:45:37.560
than in the--

00:45:37.560 --> 00:45:42.300
river than in the center
because of the friction

00:45:42.300 --> 00:45:46.440
associated with the
base of the river.

00:45:46.440 --> 00:45:51.960
And so you can use a
coefficient just to correct,

00:45:51.960 --> 00:45:54.180
and then you get your flow rate.

00:45:54.180 --> 00:45:54.720
Yay.

00:45:54.720 --> 00:45:56.160
So that was a lot
of work, and all

00:45:56.160 --> 00:46:00.600
you have is a flow rate for
one day, and water varies.

00:46:00.600 --> 00:46:03.370
Like, flow rate varies
tremendously during the season.

00:46:03.370 --> 00:46:06.210
So again, you can do
your 10-year study,

00:46:06.210 --> 00:46:10.110
or you can just get some
data on annual precipitation

00:46:10.110 --> 00:46:12.060
and when is the rainy
season, the dry season,

00:46:12.060 --> 00:46:14.250
and do your best to estimate.

00:46:14.250 --> 00:46:18.780
But, yeah, so that's how
you'd get your flow rate.

00:46:18.780 --> 00:46:22.080
And then, in terms of head, the
simplest method that, again,

00:46:22.080 --> 00:46:26.880
that I found that's
used is take a yardstick

00:46:26.880 --> 00:46:29.280
or a stick of known length.

00:46:29.280 --> 00:46:31.110
It doesn't need
to be a yardstick.

00:46:31.110 --> 00:46:33.510
And you put on it
just a tube that's

00:46:33.510 --> 00:46:37.200
clear that has the ends capped
that's half full of water.

00:46:37.200 --> 00:46:42.172
And that's a really
cheap level, basically.

00:46:42.172 --> 00:46:44.380
Or you can get a real level
if you have a real level.

00:46:44.380 --> 00:46:45.300
But if you don't
have a real level,

00:46:45.300 --> 00:46:46.980
you can just use a cheap level.

00:46:46.980 --> 00:46:49.770
And then, hopefully,
with two people,

00:46:49.770 --> 00:46:51.810
you put the stick at
the bottom or the top

00:46:51.810 --> 00:46:55.470
and then just keep moving
it over and over again

00:46:55.470 --> 00:46:58.110
until you measure your
head, which is a pain,

00:46:58.110 --> 00:47:00.630
but people do it, and it works.

00:47:00.630 --> 00:47:04.418
And actual surveying
equipment is really expensive,

00:47:04.418 --> 00:47:06.460
and so you're unlikely to
have that in the field.

00:47:06.460 --> 00:47:08.970
So if your partner
asked about this,

00:47:08.970 --> 00:47:13.690
you have a method to
measure that's plausible.

00:47:13.690 --> 00:47:14.190
Yeah?

00:47:14.190 --> 00:47:17.610
AUDIENCE: Sorry, I thought the
head was the [INAUDIBLE] water.

00:47:17.610 --> 00:47:18.990
AMY BANZAERT: Right.

00:47:18.990 --> 00:47:21.525
