this in the river perpendicular

00:47:21.525 --> 00:47:22.192
to [INAUDIBLE]--

00:47:22.192 --> 00:47:26.190
AMY BANZAERT: No, so for
the flow measurement,

00:47:26.190 --> 00:47:30.810
you're putting your
floats in the water

00:47:30.810 --> 00:47:33.360
and watching how long it
takes to get from one end

00:47:33.360 --> 00:47:36.390
to the other, and that'll
give you a measure of flow.

00:47:36.390 --> 00:47:40.990
And then your river ideally is--

00:47:40.990 --> 00:47:44.325
let me get a picture instead of
trying to draw a bad picture.

00:47:51.050 --> 00:47:53.960
So your river is hypothetically
going down the mountain

00:47:53.960 --> 00:47:56.550
here and sort of doing a curve.

00:47:56.550 --> 00:47:58.160
And so you're
saying, OK, well, I'm

00:47:58.160 --> 00:48:03.080
going to put my weir
and everything up here,

00:48:03.080 --> 00:48:06.800
and I'm going to put
my location where

00:48:06.800 --> 00:48:10.340
I'm going to actually
generate the power down here.

00:48:10.340 --> 00:48:13.100
So I need to calculate the
height from right here to right

00:48:13.100 --> 00:48:14.550
here to get my head height.

00:48:14.550 --> 00:48:19.610
So now I need to very painfully
go up the mountain in order

00:48:19.610 --> 00:48:23.010
to figure out exactly how
high this distance is.

00:48:23.010 --> 00:48:24.960
Is that cleared up?

00:48:24.960 --> 00:48:25.460
OK.

00:48:36.400 --> 00:48:40.690
OK, so that wraps
up micro hydro.

