I'm just trying to help you
get through the next couple

00:55:50.700 --> 00:55:53.880
of weeks in a way that you
actually get output and don't

00:55:53.880 --> 00:55:55.620
save it all to the
last night, which

00:55:55.620 --> 00:55:58.530
will lead to really
weak both packing

00:55:58.530 --> 00:56:02.170
and help for your
community partner.

00:56:02.170 --> 00:56:03.960
So this is all
hopefully in preparation

00:56:03.960 --> 00:56:07.140
so that you can maximize
the value to you

00:56:07.140 --> 00:56:09.750
and your community partner
for the spring break trip.

00:56:09.750 --> 00:56:11.280
And for people who
aren't traveling,

00:56:11.280 --> 00:56:13.830
you're helping make sure
they're maximizing their work,

00:56:13.830 --> 00:56:16.200
and it's also getting you
prepared for doing the project

00:56:16.200 --> 00:56:18.340
work after the spring break.

00:56:18.340 --> 00:56:21.960
So I don't think this is
busy work for you in any way.

00:56:24.480 --> 00:56:26.813
So that's one, is doing
a brainstorming effort,

00:56:26.813 --> 00:56:28.230
and I'm going to
talk a little bit

00:56:28.230 --> 00:56:30.090
about brainstorming
in a minute so you

00:56:30.090 --> 00:56:31.320
have some guidelines there.

00:56:31.320 --> 00:56:32.200
Yeah?

00:56:32.200 --> 00:56:33.870
AUDIENCE: By
pictures, do you mean

00:56:33.870 --> 00:56:37.667
find examples of existing
technology or do sketches?

00:56:37.667 --> 00:56:39.000
AMY BANZAERT: No, just sketches.

00:56:39.000 --> 00:56:42.640
So yeah, so basically, I'll
show you this in a minute.

00:56:42.640 --> 00:56:44.970
But when you do a brainstorm--

00:56:44.970 --> 00:56:48.450
like, if I was
brainstorming wind turbines,

00:56:48.450 --> 00:56:52.830
I might do a picture
that looks like that.

00:56:52.830 --> 00:56:55.035
It takes me 10 seconds
to draw it, tops.

00:56:57.870 --> 00:56:59.370
And that would be
really appropriate

00:56:59.370 --> 00:57:00.660
in our brainstorming session.

00:57:00.660 --> 00:57:02.820
For the pictures
you turn in, I would

00:57:02.820 --> 00:57:04.690
want it to be a
little more refined

00:57:04.690 --> 00:57:10.170
so that everything is drawn
a little bit more carefully.

00:57:10.170 --> 00:57:12.570
There's a few more
elements that actually make

00:57:12.570 --> 00:57:16.020
this a real device rather
than just something

00:57:16.020 --> 00:57:18.630
disembodied an air,
whatever's appropriate.

00:57:21.530 --> 00:57:25.550
Not many, if any
of you, are artists

00:57:25.550 --> 00:57:27.410
or technical graphic designers.

00:57:27.410 --> 00:57:30.770
And so I'm not looking
for an amazing sketch

00:57:30.770 --> 00:57:33.920
that was done by a graphic
designer or artist.

00:57:33.920 --> 00:57:35.130
I'm not one either.

00:57:35.130 --> 00:57:40.070
But if you just draw
relatively slowly and carefully

00:57:40.070 --> 00:57:41.720
and maybe do a
couple of tries, you

00:57:41.720 --> 00:57:44.510
should be able to get a drawing
that looks decent enough

00:57:44.510 --> 00:57:47.450
that it's understandable, which
is all I'm really looking for.

00:57:49.970 --> 00:57:52.850
So then the second thing
is a literature research

00:57:52.850 --> 00:57:55.620
on the topic, which you
may have time for as well.

00:57:55.620 --> 00:57:58.040
But I want you to do a
brainstorm initially just

00:57:58.040 --> 00:58:03.980
to get creative before you know
what's out there, necessarily.

00:58:03.980 --> 00:58:06.560
So that's why we're doing
one, brainstorming, first.

00:58:06.560 --> 00:58:09.470
But then a second set,
which you may have time

00:58:09.470 --> 00:58:14.255
to do some today if you brought
laptops or what have you

00:58:14.255 --> 00:58:18.500
is to get some
information on what's

00:58:18.500 --> 00:58:22.730
out there so that you're
more informed about whatever

00:58:22.730 --> 00:58:26.460
you need to be, whatever
your project is.

00:58:26.460 --> 00:58:29.870
And so what I think would
be a good deliverable

00:58:29.870 --> 00:58:32.450
for this is an
annotated bibliography,

00:58:32.450 --> 00:58:33.890
and there's
guidelines right there

00:58:33.890 --> 00:58:37.040
on how to write an
annotated bibliography.

00:58:37.040 --> 00:58:39.020
The guidelines for
annotated biographies

00:58:39.020 --> 00:58:42.300
are generally on the
liberal arts side of things.

00:58:42.300 --> 00:58:44.660
And so they talk
about summarizing

00:58:44.660 --> 00:58:47.463
what was the content, reflecting
on whether it was useful,

00:58:47.463 --> 00:58:49.130
and assessing the
quality of the source.

00:58:49.130 --> 00:58:51.110
And all those things
are good things,

00:58:51.110 --> 00:58:52.880
but basically I
want you to focus

00:58:52.880 --> 00:58:54.740
on summarizing what was
there and reflecting

00:58:54.740 --> 00:58:57.920
on how it's relevant to your
project for each annotation

00:58:57.920 --> 00:59:00.440
and assessing the
quality of the source.

00:59:00.440 --> 00:59:02.360
If it's a crappy source,
just don't include it

00:59:02.360 --> 00:59:06.183
in the bibliography, and if
it's a great source, include it.

00:59:06.183 --> 00:59:08.600
And if there is something to
assess that you want to note,

00:59:08.600 --> 00:59:09.980
don't hesitate to.

00:59:09.980 --> 00:59:11.480
But otherwise, you
don't really have

00:59:11.480 --> 00:59:17.070
to spend time assessing the
source in a written form.

00:59:17.070 --> 00:59:19.760
So third is developing
project specifications,

00:59:19.760 --> 00:59:22.880
and 2.009 does a really
nice job of defining what

00:59:22.880 --> 00:59:24.230
a project specification is.

00:59:24.230 --> 00:59:28.460
So I provided links to
their lecture and examples.

00:59:28.460 --> 00:59:30.800
And so you're going to
provide project specifications

00:59:30.800 --> 00:59:33.530
for each project using
the 2.009 format.

00:59:33.530 --> 00:59:37.460
So that would mean an attribute,
a metric, a unit, and a value,

00:59:37.460 --> 00:59:39.260
and you'll see the
definitions there.

00:59:39.260 --> 00:59:40.820
The value is an actual number.

00:59:40.820 --> 00:59:42.950
Like, it needs a 3 or a 7.

00:59:42.950 --> 00:59:44.660
And a lot of those
numbers, you probably

00:59:44.660 --> 00:59:47.000
won't be able to fill in
until after your trip,

00:59:47.000 --> 00:59:47.765
and that's fine.

00:59:47.765 --> 00:59:51.447
But what I would like to do
is come up with a best guess,

00:59:51.447 --> 00:59:53.780
even if it's a range of a
couple of orders of magnitude.

00:59:53.780 --> 00:59:55.430
Come up with a best
guess so that when

00:59:55.430 --> 00:59:58.370
you talk to your community
partner, who often don't have

00:59:58.370 --> 01:00:02.147
a lot of comfort with numbers
and may give you really

01:00:02.147 --> 01:00:03.980
wacky numbers, if they're
giving you numbers

01:00:03.980 --> 01:00:06.290
that are completely different
from what you're expecting,

01:00:06.290 --> 01:00:07.790
that's a really good
indicator to you

01:00:07.790 --> 01:00:09.415
that you need to keep
asking questions.

01:00:09.415 --> 01:00:11.960
So that either you learn that
your assumptions are totally

01:00:11.960 --> 01:00:14.595
off, which is a really
important thing to learn,

01:00:14.595 --> 01:00:16.970
or that your community partner
isn't really understanding

01:00:16.970 --> 01:00:21.285
the question or doesn't have
the educational background

01:00:21.285 --> 01:00:22.910
to be able to give
you a useful number,

01:00:22.910 --> 01:00:25.490
and you have to work in
a few different guises

01:00:25.490 --> 01:00:26.910
to actually get to that number.

01:00:26.910 --> 01:00:28.820
And we'll talk about
that on the trip prep

01:00:28.820 --> 01:00:31.970
week of how you actually
do that process of getting

01:00:31.970 --> 01:00:32.930
that information.

01:00:35.910 --> 01:00:40.850
And then the next aspects are
doing a second brainstorm.

01:00:40.850 --> 01:00:43.670
And it's up to you
as a team if you

01:00:43.670 --> 01:00:45.055
want to do this in two stages.

01:00:45.055 --> 01:00:47.180
So sometimes people like
to brainstorm on their own

01:00:47.180 --> 01:00:49.700
for a bit and then come
together and bring those ideas

01:00:49.700 --> 01:00:53.100
to a brainstorm and also build
on more in a group brainstorm

01:00:53.100 --> 01:00:53.600
session.

01:00:53.600 --> 01:00:55.820
Some people prefer just to do
a group brainstorming session.

01:00:55.820 --> 01:00:56.632
So it's up to you.

01:00:56.632 --> 01:00:58.340
But the second brainstorm
should at least

01:00:58.340 --> 01:00:59.930
have a group component.

01:00:59.930 --> 01:01:02.930
And for those, it's just
two annotated pictures

01:01:02.930 --> 01:01:04.100
for each project.

01:01:04.100 --> 01:01:07.580
And so it's fewer
pictures because it's not

01:01:07.580 --> 01:01:08.640
per team member.

01:01:08.640 --> 01:01:10.700
And so you guys
can just figure out

01:01:10.700 --> 01:01:12.680
how you want to split those up.

01:01:12.680 --> 01:01:15.310
Another thing is the
big questions you have

01:01:15.310 --> 01:01:17.810
that you want to make sure you
get answered so that you have

01:01:17.810 --> 01:01:19.935
those in mind so that you
make sure that by the end

01:01:19.935 --> 01:01:23.390
of the trip you've gotten
answers to those questions.

01:01:23.390 --> 01:01:25.880
And all of these questions
are related to figuring out,

01:01:25.880 --> 01:01:27.750
can you actually
work on this project?

01:01:27.750 --> 01:01:28.880
Is this a good project?

01:01:28.880 --> 01:01:31.250
And if you are going
to work on it, what

01:01:31.250 --> 01:01:33.680
is the appropriate deliverable?

01:01:33.680 --> 01:01:35.480
That's the type of questions.

01:01:35.480 --> 01:01:38.210
A materials list for the
trip-- so you may well

01:01:38.210 --> 01:01:39.710
need some materials.

01:01:39.710 --> 01:01:41.780
If you're going to do a
charcoal demonstration,

01:01:41.780 --> 01:01:44.000
if you're going to try to
build a washing machine,

01:01:44.000 --> 01:01:46.660
if you have a great
idea for a project,

01:01:46.660 --> 01:01:49.160
and you want to try to actually
implement either a prototype

01:01:49.160 --> 01:01:51.632
or something in the field,
your community partner

01:01:51.632 --> 01:01:53.840
needs to know so that they
can provide the materials,

01:01:53.840 --> 01:01:56.670
and/or we need to get them here
so that we can bring them down.

01:01:56.670 --> 01:02:01.580
And so I put that
you don't need this

01:02:01.580 --> 01:02:04.190
until the 16th, which is
right before the trip,

01:02:04.190 --> 01:02:06.500
and that's only if--

01:02:06.500 --> 01:02:10.850
like, that's the last desperate
time if you can define them

01:02:10.850 --> 01:02:13.490
sooner, that's better, so that
your community partner has time

01:02:13.490 --> 01:02:17.975
to get them, and/or we have time
to get them if you need help.

01:02:17.975 --> 01:02:19.100
Oh, no, that was questions.

01:02:19.100 --> 01:02:19.975
Materials is earlier.

01:02:19.975 --> 01:02:20.830
OK, good.

01:02:20.830 --> 01:02:22.640
That makes more sense.

01:02:22.640 --> 01:02:24.730
And then, finally,
on the 16th, you're

01:02:24.730 --> 01:02:30.190
going to do about a 15-minute
presentation on literature

01:02:30.190 --> 01:02:32.290
search findings, your
project specifications,

01:02:32.290 --> 01:02:34.420
the annotated pictures,
the key questions,

01:02:34.420 --> 01:02:37.090
and your key expected activities
just so that you're well

01:02:37.090 --> 01:02:38.950
prepared for the trip.

01:02:38.950 --> 01:02:42.010
So that's basically
the assignments

01:02:42.010 --> 01:02:45.140
apart from a little bit of
reading for the next few weeks,

01:02:45.140 --> 01:02:46.850
which, again,
violates the syllabus

01:02:46.850 --> 01:02:52.335
but hopefully will work
out or be OK with guys.

01:02:52.335 --> 01:02:54.460
But I don't want to add
problem sets on top of this

01:02:54.460 --> 01:02:56.440
because this is a lot of work.

01:02:56.440 --> 01:02:59.560
Are there any questions
about all of this?

01:02:59.560 --> 01:03:02.260
I have it printed
out so that when

01:03:02.260 --> 01:03:07.080
you're working on it today,
you have something to refer to.

01:03:07.080 --> 01:03:07.640
Yeah.

01:03:07.640 --> 01:03:10.850
AUDIENCE: Did you get the
dates for the places you had?

01:03:10.850 --> 01:03:12.200
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

01:03:12.200 --> 01:03:16.760
So most people are flying on--

01:03:16.760 --> 01:03:20.930
so people going to Nicaragua
are flying on Sunday

01:03:20.930 --> 01:03:24.740
and getting back on
the following Sunday.

01:03:24.740 --> 01:03:26.570
What about El Salvador?

01:03:26.570 --> 01:03:31.890
So Juan and Jessica have
personal commitments,

01:03:31.890 --> 01:03:35.150
so they're flying
back on Friday.

01:03:39.820 --> 01:03:42.160
And do you know what El
Salvador is doing yet, Amit?

01:03:42.160 --> 01:03:45.612
AMIT GANDHI: I think it's
Saturday [INAUDIBLE]..

01:03:45.612 --> 01:03:46.320
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

01:03:46.320 --> 01:03:50.308
So El Salvador maybe
traveling a day earlier.

01:03:50.308 --> 01:03:50.808
So--

01:03:50.808 --> 01:03:57.060
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
whatever the days are.

01:03:57.060 --> 01:03:59.750
AMY BANZAERT: I think so.

01:03:59.750 --> 01:04:03.830
Yeah, the 18th is the Friday,
so the Sunday would be the 20th.

01:04:03.830 --> 01:04:06.738
And we'll send out your
actual itinerary soon.

01:04:06.738 --> 01:04:08.030
Some of the tickets are booked.

01:04:08.030 --> 01:04:11.790
Others are just reserved, and
we'll be booking them today.

01:04:11.790 --> 01:04:14.300
So there's a couple
of reading assignments

01:04:14.300 --> 01:04:17.790
that'll be up on
Stellar shortly.

01:04:17.790 --> 01:04:20.060
This is just a somewhat
different perspective

01:04:20.060 --> 01:04:22.640
from the Paul Polak
reading you did.

01:04:22.640 --> 01:04:26.480
And then this is directions
on charcoal making.

01:04:26.480 --> 01:04:29.090
So both are quite short.

01:04:29.090 --> 01:04:31.520
So I know it might be
seeming like a lot,

01:04:31.520 --> 01:04:36.710
but these are very
manageable to get done.

01:04:36.710 --> 01:04:41.570
So now I just want to go
over how to do brainstorming,

01:04:41.570 --> 01:04:43.250
and then we can take
a break, and then

01:04:43.250 --> 01:04:44.750
you can get into
your project groups

01:04:44.750 --> 01:04:47.090
and do brainstorming and
start talking about the trip.

01:04:47.090 --> 01:04:51.035
And then at 2:00, your
mentors will show up,

01:04:51.035 --> 01:04:52.160
unless your mentor is Amit.

01:04:52.160 --> 01:04:55.970
He's already here--
your trip leader,

01:04:55.970 --> 01:05:00.000
and so you can get
support from them as well

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:01.740
and get to know them.

01:05:01.740 --> 01:05:06.800
So effective and
fun brainstorming,

01:05:06.800 --> 01:05:09.330
you require a clear
problem statement.

01:05:09.330 --> 01:05:18.290
So for this case, it's
whatever the project idea

01:05:18.290 --> 01:05:21.500
is for your community
partner project.

01:05:21.500 --> 01:05:24.980
After you get back
from spring break,

01:05:24.980 --> 01:05:26.690
you have about four
weeks of building,

01:05:26.690 --> 01:05:28.640
and you have about
a $500 budget.

01:05:28.640 --> 01:05:31.340
So while you'll see
I'm going to encourage

01:05:31.340 --> 01:05:35.240
you to have really wild
ideas, and so if you come up

01:05:35.240 --> 01:05:37.640
with a idea that completely
violates the four

01:05:37.640 --> 01:05:39.320
weeks of building
in the $500 budget,

01:05:39.320 --> 01:05:40.830
still throw it out there.

01:05:40.830 --> 01:05:44.210
It's nice to have in your mind
a general sense of these sorts

01:05:44.210 --> 01:05:47.720
of things so that you're not
only brainstorming things that

01:05:47.720 --> 01:05:51.530
are in the 7-megawatt
wind turbine category

01:05:51.530 --> 01:05:53.030
if you're thinking
about wind power.

01:05:56.270 --> 01:06:00.910
So for fun and
effective brainstorming,

01:06:00.910 --> 01:06:02.740
you're trying to
generate as many ideas

01:06:02.740 --> 01:06:04.885
as you can in 15 minutes.

01:06:10.270 --> 01:06:13.740
So 15 minutes is a
good length of time.

01:06:13.740 --> 01:06:15.960
Sometimes you need a
little more, a little less,

01:06:15.960 --> 01:06:18.990
but around that time for any
particular project statement.

01:06:18.990 --> 01:06:21.390
And then you can, say, take
a break, take a stretch,

01:06:21.390 --> 01:06:24.450
and then come back and work
on the next problem statement.

01:06:24.450 --> 01:06:28.260
You want your sketch to really
be a picture with a few words.

01:06:28.260 --> 01:06:35.190
So a lot of people, if they're
brainstorming weapons of war,

01:06:35.190 --> 01:06:37.620
and you have your
8-and-1/2 piece of paper,

01:06:37.620 --> 01:06:40.545
might draw this.

01:06:48.150 --> 01:06:52.190
And I would say that this
picture is a lot more evocative

01:06:52.190 --> 01:06:56.930
of what your idea is than
using the words big and just

01:06:56.930 --> 01:06:57.980
a tiny drawing.

01:06:57.980 --> 01:07:00.710
And those of us who are
not so good at drawing--

01:07:00.710 --> 01:07:02.180
and I'm one of them--

01:07:02.180 --> 01:07:04.520
are tempted to draw a really
small because you don't

01:07:04.520 --> 01:07:07.460
want to show in
really big format

01:07:07.460 --> 01:07:08.780
just how bad your drawing is.

01:07:08.780 --> 01:07:11.570
But even being a
weak drawer, you

01:07:11.570 --> 01:07:14.300
can still get your idea
across pretty well.

01:07:14.300 --> 01:07:17.270
This is, like, a 3-minute
sketch for an artist.

01:07:17.270 --> 01:07:21.620
So none of your brainstorm ideas
are going to look this crafted.

01:07:21.620 --> 01:07:24.230
This might be the
actual annotated sketch

01:07:24.230 --> 01:07:26.810
that you turn in that you
actually refine and spend

01:07:26.810 --> 01:07:27.740
some time on.

01:07:27.740 --> 01:07:34.610
So the 10-second version
is going to be a guy,

01:07:34.610 --> 01:07:37.910
and you have a guy, and
he's shooting stuff.

01:07:37.910 --> 01:07:42.620
And here's the person who's
unhappy getting shot at.

01:07:42.620 --> 01:07:47.000
And then here's the wheel
that's moving your wall of doom,

01:07:47.000 --> 01:07:47.510
right?

01:07:47.510 --> 01:07:50.240
So this is a really fast
version of that sketch,

01:07:50.240 --> 01:07:53.490
and so that's what I'm
looking for, not that.

01:07:53.490 --> 01:07:57.440
But I just wanted to emphasize
most people start out

01:07:57.440 --> 01:08:00.500
doing this, and you really want
to force yourself to do that

01:08:00.500 --> 01:08:03.320
with maybe the description
at the top or the bottom,

01:08:03.320 --> 01:08:05.460
but the picture should
dominate, not the words.

01:08:05.460 --> 01:08:05.960
Yeah.

01:08:05.960 --> 01:08:07.877
AUDIENCE: Yeah, so I'm
still a little confused

01:08:07.877 --> 01:08:12.050
as to how the three projects
that you listed on each slide

01:08:12.050 --> 01:08:14.360
relate to the
brainstorming [INAUDIBLE]..

01:08:14.360 --> 01:08:18.890
AMY BANZAERT: OK, yeah,
so the three projects-- so

01:08:18.890 --> 01:08:21.770
if one of your projects was
the feed chopper, right now,

01:08:21.770 --> 01:08:24.200
you know nothing about
that project, right?

01:08:24.200 --> 01:08:26.444
So you may not be able
to brainstorm that

01:08:26.444 --> 01:08:28.069
at all until you come
talk to me, which

01:08:28.069 --> 01:08:30.529
is part of what you need
to do in the next hour

01:08:30.529 --> 01:08:33.020
and a half is come
talk to me, so I

01:08:33.020 --> 01:08:34.700
can give each team
a little bit more

01:08:34.700 --> 01:08:37.109
detail about their
three projects.

01:08:37.109 --> 01:08:39.470
But one of them
might be something

01:08:39.470 --> 01:08:42.560
you can brainstorm on
initially if there's

01:08:42.560 --> 01:08:43.640
a queue to wait for me.

01:08:43.640 --> 01:08:46.712
But either way, once a little
bit about the feed chopper,

01:08:46.712 --> 01:08:48.920
then that'll be one of your
brainstorming assignments

01:08:48.920 --> 01:08:52.100
is the project, the
community partner,

01:08:52.100 --> 01:08:56.420
needs this mechanization
of the feed chopper,

01:08:56.420 --> 01:09:00.470
and let's brainstorm ways to
mechanize that feed chopper.

01:09:00.470 --> 01:09:04.520
And just put out as many ideas
as you can in 15 minutes.

01:09:04.520 --> 01:09:10.550
And a good goal is to be
generating at least three ideas

01:09:10.550 --> 01:09:12.810
per minute, which doesn't
sound like that many,

01:09:12.810 --> 01:09:15.140
but it's pretty challenging.

01:09:15.140 --> 01:09:18.200
But that is something
to keep in your mind is,

01:09:18.200 --> 01:09:20.550
I should be generating
three ideas per minute.

01:09:20.550 --> 01:09:24.560
And so if I'm not, I need
to get crazier, probably,

01:09:24.560 --> 01:09:27.600
and less inhibited in order
to successfully do it.

01:09:27.600 --> 01:09:31.729
And so once you draw
this beautiful picture

01:09:31.729 --> 01:09:35.810
with your group, what you do is
everyone's hopefully drawing.

01:09:35.810 --> 01:09:37.520
And you just say,
hey, guys, my idea

01:09:37.520 --> 01:09:40.550
is an advancing wall of doom.

01:09:40.550 --> 01:09:42.260
And then you grab
a piece of tape,

01:09:42.260 --> 01:09:47.120
and we're going to use the
three fume hoods as our boards.

01:09:47.120 --> 01:09:49.640
So each group will be
around a fume hood.

01:09:49.640 --> 01:09:51.740
And so just tape your
idea up on the wall

01:09:51.740 --> 01:09:53.240
and then get back on drawing.

01:09:53.240 --> 01:09:57.410
And if-- Curt, you had said
this advancing wall of doom,

01:09:57.410 --> 01:10:01.250
and I love that idea,
I can say, yeah,

01:10:01.250 --> 01:10:03.140
it's going to be an
advancing wall of doom.

01:10:03.140 --> 01:10:05.720
But instead of
being wheel-powered,

01:10:05.720 --> 01:10:08.203
it's going to be on--

01:10:08.203 --> 01:10:09.620
on a big wheel
powered, it's going

01:10:09.620 --> 01:10:11.240
to be on a bunch
of little wheels.

01:10:11.240 --> 01:10:14.910
And so in other circles, that
might be considered theft,

01:10:14.910 --> 01:10:15.410
right?

01:10:15.410 --> 01:10:17.618
And you'd be really mad at
me for stealing your idea.

01:10:17.618 --> 01:10:21.440
But in brainstorming, that's
really encouraged is to, quote,

01:10:21.440 --> 01:10:22.500
"steal" people's ideas.

01:10:22.500 --> 01:10:26.000
So have no worry about that.

01:10:26.000 --> 01:10:27.710
You guys can work out
your patent rights

01:10:27.710 --> 01:10:31.890
if you become millionaires over
your advancing wall of doom

01:10:31.890 --> 01:10:32.390
later.

01:10:35.100 --> 01:10:37.290
So what are the
rules of the game?

01:10:37.290 --> 01:10:39.870
So one is deferred judgment.

01:10:39.870 --> 01:10:44.930
So when I propose that I want to
make a wind turbine that's just

01:10:44.930 --> 01:10:48.650
going to float in the air
magically with helium balloons,

01:10:48.650 --> 01:10:52.290
and you see a few
flaws in that idea,

01:10:52.290 --> 01:10:54.800
you just have to bite
your tongue, right?

01:10:54.800 --> 01:10:57.810
And the one way you can
manage that is by saying,

01:10:57.810 --> 01:11:00.800
OK, I'm going to
build on your idea.

01:11:00.800 --> 01:11:02.660
And instead of helium
balloons, we're

01:11:02.660 --> 01:11:06.710
going to use a post that's
attached to the ground.

01:11:06.710 --> 01:11:10.910
But deferred judgment, even of
your own ideas and of anyone

01:11:10.910 --> 01:11:11.930
else's.

01:11:11.930 --> 01:11:14.810
If you're showing your idea, no
saying, this is really stupid,

01:11:14.810 --> 01:11:15.440
but here it is.

01:11:15.440 --> 01:11:16.913
Just say what it is.

01:11:16.913 --> 01:11:19.580
Hopefully, everyone is coming up
with some really terrible ideas

01:11:19.580 --> 01:11:21.507
as well as really good ones.

01:11:21.507 --> 01:11:23.090
And some of those
really terrible ones

01:11:23.090 --> 01:11:24.960
have a kernel of
awesomeness in them,

01:11:24.960 --> 01:11:27.260
and so that's why it's
worth it to get them out.

01:11:27.260 --> 01:11:28.230
One at a time--

01:11:28.230 --> 01:11:31.890
so if everyone is talking
at once, it's ineffective.

01:11:31.890 --> 01:11:33.620
So post them up one
at a time, and you

01:11:33.620 --> 01:11:36.860
shouldn't draw three ideas
on one piece of paper.

01:11:36.860 --> 01:11:40.160
You should have each piece
of paper for each idea.

01:11:40.160 --> 01:11:44.330
As I mentioned, so building an
ideas, so theft is encouraged.

01:11:44.330 --> 01:11:46.370
Don't be afraid of that.

01:11:46.370 --> 01:11:48.080
Try to stay focused,
which can be hard.

01:11:48.080 --> 01:11:50.420
Spending 15 minutes
trying to generate

01:11:50.420 --> 01:11:53.810
three ideas per minute--
that's a lot of ideas, but do

01:11:53.810 --> 01:11:54.930
try to do that.

01:11:54.930 --> 01:11:59.270
And then, as I
mentioned, be visual.

01:11:59.270 --> 01:12:01.820
And encourage-- use wild ideas.

01:12:01.820 --> 01:12:04.272
Don't be afraid to be like,
oh, yeah, wall of doom,

01:12:04.272 --> 01:12:06.230
but it's going to be made
out of peanut butter.

01:12:06.230 --> 01:12:10.308
Like, something that ridiculous
just pops in your head,

01:12:10.308 --> 01:12:12.350
throw it out there because
it may inspire someone

01:12:12.350 --> 01:12:15.020
to say, well, not peanut butter,
but what about peanut shells?

01:12:15.020 --> 01:12:17.270
That would be a really
low-cost way to make it, right?

01:12:17.270 --> 01:12:19.550
And all of a sudden that's
slightly useful idea

01:12:19.550 --> 01:12:21.680
for this ridiculous
wall of doom.

01:12:21.680 --> 01:12:25.370
Yeah, and then once
you're done, you

01:12:25.370 --> 01:12:32.630
can sort the pile to divide--

01:12:32.630 --> 01:12:33.950
to get them into themes.

01:12:33.950 --> 01:12:38.090
So you can just figure
out what are your themes

01:12:38.090 --> 01:12:40.610
or what are your combinations.

01:12:40.610 --> 01:12:42.810
Figure out, do those
themes actually make sense?

01:12:42.810 --> 01:12:45.420
And then I'll often refine
and combine the best.

01:12:45.420 --> 01:12:46.898
So those two ideas
from each group

01:12:46.898 --> 01:12:49.190
don't necessarily have to be
the ones you came up with,

01:12:49.190 --> 01:12:51.890
but ones that you think
are some of the best

01:12:51.890 --> 01:12:53.660
of what you came up with.

01:12:53.660 --> 01:12:55.310
Do you guys have
questions on that?

01:12:55.310 --> 01:12:56.772
I went over that kind of fast.

01:12:59.970 --> 01:13:02.160
But, yeah, OK.

01:13:02.160 --> 01:13:06.390
So then for the next
hour and a half,

01:13:06.390 --> 01:13:08.700
you guys can take
your 10-minute break

01:13:08.700 --> 01:13:12.780
and then come back and do the
brainstorming, meet with me.

01:13:12.780 --> 01:13:15.180
And then please remember to
fill out your money cards

01:13:15.180 --> 01:13:21.420
and include the hours per
week on them that you think

01:13:21.420 --> 01:13:23.460
you're spending on the
class so that I just have

01:13:23.460 --> 01:13:24.627
a sense of how that's going.

01:13:30.680 --> 01:13:32.180
AUDIENCE: But it's
a big blue box

01:13:32.180 --> 01:13:34.580
